---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Web vs. desktop — sudonotes docs"
  description="The planned browser build of sudonotes, how connecting to a local vault differs from importing a copy, and which browsers can do it."
  type="article"
>
  <section class="page prose" style="padding-block: clamp(44px, 7vw, 76px)">
    <p class="eyebrow">Docs</p>
    <h1>Web vs. desktop</h1>

    <div class="note warn">
      <strong>The browser app does not exist yet.</strong> This page describes the design being
      built, so the distinction below is clear before anyone relies on it. Today, sudonotes is the
      desktop app only.
    </div>

    <h2>Two different things, never blurred</h2>
    <p>
      A browser can be given access to a real folder on your disk. That is the whole basis of the
      planned browser app — and it comes with a hard split that will be labelled plainly in the UI:
    </p>

    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Mode</th><th>What happens</th><th>Shares a vault with desktop?</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Connect existing vault</strong></td>
            <td>Reads and writes your original <code>.md</code> files in place.</td>
            <td class="yes">Yes</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Import a copy</strong></td>
            <td>Copies notes into browser storage.</td>
            <td class="no">No — you export to get them back out</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Browser support</h2>
    <p>
      Connecting needs the File System Access API, which not every browser has. Where it is missing,
      import and export still work — you just do not get a live shared vault.
    </p>

    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Browser</th><th>Connect to a vault</th><th>Import / export</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Chrome, Edge</strong></td><td class="yes">Yes</td><td class="yes">Yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Firefox</strong></td><td class="no">No</td><td class="yes">Yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Safari</strong></td><td class="no">No</td><td class="yes">Yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Mobile browsers</strong></td><td class="no">No</td><td class="yes">Yes</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Things the desktop app will keep to itself</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Project linking.</strong> Writing an <code>IDEAS.md</code> into an arbitrary repository, and editing its <code>.gitignore</code>, is not something a browser should be able to do.</li>
      <li><strong>Live file watching.</strong> The desktop app notices external edits within about a third of a second. A browser cannot watch a folder, so it will rescan when you return to the tab, plus a manual rescan button.</li>
      <li><strong>The search index.</strong> <code>.sudonotes/index.db</code> is the desktop app's. The browser will build its own index in memory, and its result ranking will differ. That is a real difference, not a rounding error, and it is stated here rather than papered over.</li>
      <li><strong>Native window chrome</strong> and absolute path editing.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why this is the hard part</h2>
    <p>
      Two programs writing into one folder must agree, byte for byte, on how a note is serialized —
      the frontmatter, the filename slug, the collision rules. If they disagree even slightly they
      will quietly rewrite each other's files every time you switch. The approach is to compile the
      same Rust code the desktop app uses to WebAssembly and have the browser call it, so the two
      cannot drift apart by construction.
    </p>
    <p>
      Both sides will also fingerprint a note when it is opened and re-check before writing. If it
      changed underneath you, you get a choice — reload, keep yours, or save a copy — never a silent
      overwrite.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
